The bug is in the Unicode supplementary-character handling in several `StringUtils` search methods, with the clearest root cause visible in:

- `StringUtils.indexOfAny(CharSequence, char[])`
- `StringUtils.containsAny(CharSequence, char[])`
- `StringUtils.containsNone(CharSequence, char[])`
- `StringUtils.indexOfAnyBut(CharSequence, char[])`
- `StringUtils.indexOfAnyBut(String, String)`

Diagnosis:
These methods compare `char` units independently and do not correctly distinguish full supplementary code points from shared surrogate halves.

Why this matches the failures:
- `CharU20000` and `CharU20001` are different supplementary characters, but they share the same high surrogate.
- The buggy methods often treat a match on just one `char`—especially the high surrogate—as a full character match.
- That causes:
  - `containsAny(...)` to return `true` when only half/a wrong supplementary character matched.
  - `containsNone(...)` to return `false` for the same reason.
  - `indexOfAny(...)` to return `0` instead of `2`, because it falsely matches the first character’s shared high surrogate.
  - `indexOfAnyBut(...)` to skip too far and return `3` instead of `2`, because it treats both halves of the first supplementary character as fully “contained” in the search set based on surrogate-unit matching rather than code-point matching.

Static evidence:
- `indexOfAny(CharSequence, char[])`:
  ```java
  if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
      return i;
  }
  ```
  This returns on any `char` match, with no surrogate-pair validation.

- `containsNone(CharSequence, char[])`:
  ```java
  if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
      return false;
  }
  ```
  Same issue.

- `indexOfAnyBut(CharSequence, char[])`:
  ```java
  if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
      continue outer;
  }
  ```
  Again, a single `char` match is treated as full-character containment.

- `indexOfAnyBut(String, String)`:
  ```java
  if (searchChars.indexOf(ch) < 0) {
      return i;
  }
  ```
  `String.indexOf(ch)` is also only checking a single UTF-16 code unit.

`containsAny(CharSequence, char[])` has partial surrogate logic, but it is still wrong for the “bad supplementary char” edge case because the `else` branch returns `true` on a low-surrogate match alone:
```java
if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
    if (i < csLast && j < searchLast && ch >= Character.MIN_HIGH_SURROGATE && ch <= Character.MAX_HIGH_SURROGATE) {
        if (searchChars[j + 1] == cs.charAt(i + 1)) {
            return true;
        }
    } else {
        return true;
    }
}
```
That incorrectly accepts isolated low surrogates or non-high-surrogate `char` matches that are only halves of supplementary characters.

Dynamic verification:
The JDB-based verification for `indexOfAny(CharSequence, char[])` was inconclusive because it did not hit the failing supplementary-character test case, but the static evidence aligns tightly with all reported failures.

Overall conclusion:
The buggy behavior is caused by incorrect UTF-16 surrogate handling: the methods operate on individual `char` values where they should treat supplementary characters as surrogate pairs and only report matches/non-matches when the pair is validated consistently.

